; `...` alone is no term: it marks a pattern in a set (`{1, ..., n}`). The rule for the elements of
; a set of three, `$y2 ∈ {$y1, $y2, $y3}` "element-2-of-3", matched the range `{1, ..., 3}` with
; `$y2 := ...`: `... ∈ {1, ..., 3}` and `... ∈ {5, ..., 7}` followed, and with "range" `... <= 3`
; and `5 <= ...` -- `false` (found while looking into a slow search, 2026-10-14). Now no variable
; takes `...` as its value, in the search (`unify_exprs_with_patterns`) and in the kernel.
load natural
2 ∈ {1, 2, 3}                 ; (the rules for sets of three)
expect "ProofError"
    ... ∈ {1, ..., 3}
